export const studyGuides = [
  article('small-tools-large-machines','meaning','The small tools inside the large machine','Why fractions belong on the road to spacecraft.',
    'It can feel strange to dream about space habitats and spend an evening reducing fractions. The connection is real, but it helps to make it specific. Foundations become useful when you see the job each small operation performs inside a larger decision.',
    [
      ['Follow one quantity',
        'Imagine a fictional research vehicle using 36 watts for 25 minutes. You do not need orbital mechanics to calculate its delivered energy: 36 × 25/60 = 15 watt-hours. The fraction converts minutes to hours so the rate and duration use compatible units.',
        'Several ordinary skills are working together: naming a quantity, interpreting “per,” multiplying fractions, and judging whether the result is plausible. A later energy model may vary the load and include losses, but it still needs those meanings to remain intact.'],
      ['See the next layers',
        'Algebra lets you ask the reverse question: with a stated energy allowance, how long can the load operate? Functions describe a load that changes with time. Calculus eventually handles continuous accumulation. The early work is part of that chain, rather than a separate test of patience.',
        'NASA’s Hubble description provides a real example of solar generation and battery storage supporting spacecraft operation. Our small calculation is an invented teaching model, not a Hubble sizing calculation. Keeping that distinction clear is itself an engineering habit.'],
      ['Give repetition a purpose',
        'Before a practice set, name the capability you are building: “I want to convert a duration without confusing the units.” Afterward, choose one problem and explain where that capability appears in the vehicle example. You do not need an aerospace story for every arithmetic operation.',
        'Judge progress by whether the small step becomes accurate, understandable, and usable after a delay. A page of completed exercises is evidence of activity. A quantity you can interpret and check is evidence of growing capability. Both effort and feedback are needed.']
    ],
    'Use a hypothetical 24-watt device for 40 minutes. Calculate the delivered energy and explain the factor 40/60. Check: 16 Wh. Then predict what happens if the duration doubles before doing the arithmetic.',
    'A foundation matters because it performs a job inside a larger model.',[R.power,R.curriculum]),
  article('craft-without-constant-excitement','meaning','You do not have to love every minute','Useful practice can be ordinary, repetitive, and worth doing.',
    'Some evenings of mathematics will feel satisfying. Others will feel like checking brackets and doing one more fraction. Neither feeling decides whether engineering is right for you. A serious interest can coexist with boredom, frustration, and a preference for the finished machine.',
    [
      ['Separate the destination from the task',
        'Wanting to help build a robot does not require enjoying every line of its documentation. Similarly, wanting to understand motion does not require finding every algebra exercise thrilling. The useful question is whether today’s task develops something you need and whether the difficulty is appropriate.',
        'Suppose you repeatedly lose a negative sign in −3(x − 2). A short set that makes you account for both products has a clear purpose. Repeating identical questions long after you can explain and check them may have less value than introducing a small variation.'],
      ['Practice a craft, not a performance',
        'A careful solution has visible joints: defined symbols, one justified transformation per line, and a check at the end. The satisfaction may come from making those joints cleaner. You are learning to produce work that another person can inspect and that your future self can understand.',
        'NASA’s verification processes illustrate why evidence and consistency matter in engineering. That does not turn a homework sheet into flight certification. It offers a useful analogy: trust grows when a result can withstand questions about how it was obtained.'],
      ['Adjust the practice when boredom speaks',
        'Boredom can mean the material is too easy, the purpose is unclear, the session is too long, or you have been watching instead of doing. Try one small diagnostic change: a new problem format, an explanation from memory, a shorter session, or a meaningful application.',
        'You also have permission to finish a sensible amount and stop. There is no prize for converting an ordinary study session into a demonstration of suffering. Sustainable effort means choosing work you can repeat, reviewing what it accomplished, and changing the approach when evidence calls for it.']
    ],
    'Take a repetitive skill and write its job in one sentence. Solve one routine example, one changed example, and one example containing an intentional error. Decide which version still requires useful attention.',
    'Commitment is compatible with ordinary days; purposeful practice does not require constant excitement.',[R.verification,R.start]),
  article('why-calculate-with-computers','meaning','Why learn the arithmetic if software can do it?','The machine needs someone who can ask and check the question.',
    'Computers make enormous calculations possible. That makes mathematical judgment more useful, not less. Your goal is not to beat software at multiplication. It is to recognize what the inputs mean, choose an appropriate relationship, and notice when a polished answer is unreasonable.',
    [
      ['Separate computation from interpretation',
        'A calculator will evaluate 72 ÷ 3.6 as 20. It will also evaluate 72 × 3.6 as 259.2. The tool cannot determine from those bare keystrokes whether you intended to convert kilometers per hour into meters per second. That meaning comes from your setup.',
        'Writing 72 km/h × 1,000 m/km × 1 h/3,600 s makes the intended operation inspectable. Mental estimation provides another route: 72 kilometers per hour is 1.2 kilometers each minute, which is 20 meters each second. Two representations can expose one bad input.'],
      ['Use hand work where it teaches structure',
        'During early learning, work manageable examples on paper so you can see the operations and their reasons. Once a method is understood, a calculator can handle awkward arithmetic while you concentrate on the model. In a course, follow the instructor’s rules and prepare for the tools permitted on assessments.',
        'Avoid turning every calculation into a moral test about calculator use. The question is educational: which part of this task am I trying to learn? If the objective is fraction reasoning, immediate decimal conversion can hide the structure you need to examine.'],
      ['Build a checking partnership',
        'NASA’s Mars Climate Orbiter investigation is a reminder that sophisticated systems can still fail at interfaces between quantities and units. A beginner’s response is simple: label inputs, predict scale, and keep the relation that generated an answer.',
        'When software surprises you, do not immediately assume either that it is wrong or that you are. Test a small case whose answer you know. Inspect units, angle mode, parentheses, and domain. A trustworthy workflow gives you a way to investigate disagreement instead of voting on which source feels authoritative.']
    ],
    'Before using a calculator, estimate 19.8 × 0.51. Then calculate it: 10.098. Explain why an answer near 1,000 would fail immediately and why the estimate alone cannot establish the final digits.',
    'Software performs operations; mathematical literacy helps you choose and evaluate them.',[R.units,R.ai]),
  article('fluency-and-meaning','meaning','Understanding and fluency strengthen each other','You need reasons you can explain and procedures you can use.',
    'There is no need to choose between understanding mathematics and practicing it. Knowing why a method works helps you repair it. Being able to carry it out reliably lets you use that reasoning inside a longer problem without losing track of the larger question.',
    [
      ['Give a procedure a reason',
        'For 1/3 + 1/4, the denominators name different-sized pieces. Renaming the fractions as 4/12 and 3/12 permits counting like pieces, giving 7/12. That explanation is more portable than remembering only a sequence of cross-multiplications.',
        'Now practice the same relationship with 2/3 + 1/6. A shared denominator of 6 gives 5/6. The method is related, but blindly repeating the exact numerical pattern from the first example is unnecessary. Understanding helps you see a simpler route.'],
      ['Make fluency observable',
        'Fluency here means accurate, reasonably efficient use of a familiar method, with the ability to explain important steps. It does not mean racing a timer or doing all mathematics mentally. Different topics and different learners need different amounts of practice.',
        'Look for specific evidence: you select a common denominator, preserve value while rewriting, perform the arithmetic, and catch an impossible result. If one of those steps is shaky, practice that step directly. Calling the whole subject “hard” gives you less guidance.'],
      ['Alternate support and independence',
        'IES recommends combining worked solutions with independent problem solving. In practice, study a short example, explain its decisions, close it, and attempt a related question. Use the comparison to locate the first difference that matters.',
        'Do not demand complete intuitive clarity before attempting anything, and do not treat correct imitation as complete understanding. A sensible loop moves between a reason, an action, feedback, and a new attempt. Some insight arrives while you are doing the work; some practice becomes useful only after a missing idea is explained.']
    ],
    'Solve 3/4 + 1/8, explain why the answer is 7/8, and invent a different problem using eighths. Later, solve your new problem without looking at the original explanation.',
    'Aim for methods that are both explainable and dependable.',[R.fractions,R.ies]),
  article('space-goal-with-options','meaning','Keep space in view without making it a deadline','A distant goal can guide today’s choices while the route stays adjustable.',
    'A tentative graduation target around 2033 can organize planning, but it cannot predict your exact job or who will live in space in the 2040s. You can still choose useful work now. Strong foundations preserve options across spacecraft, robotics, structures, energy, and many other engineering areas.',
    [
      ['Choose capabilities that travel',
        'The same ratio reasoning can describe a power budget, a scale drawing, or a data rate. The same algebra can rearrange a relationship in thermal analysis or robotic motion. Early mathematics is valuable partly because it remains useful when your interests become more specific.',
        'UC San Diego’s engineering overview emphasizes design, computation, and experimentation. Treat that as a picture of the kinds of work ahead, not a frozen promise about future degree requirements. Transfer planning and course availability need official checking when you make enrollment decisions.'],
      ['Distinguish contribution from destination',
        'Working on technology for space and personally flying in space are different goals. Many meaningful contributions happen on Earth. NASA’s current astronaut information emphasizes technical qualifications alongside leadership, teamwork, and communication, and selection criteria may change before you are ready to apply.',
        'No arithmetic app, project list, or chosen major can guarantee flight selection. A more useful question is whether you are becoming someone who can learn difficult systems, work reliably with others, and contribute valuable technical skill wherever the opportunity arises.'],
      ['Use the goal as a compass',
        'Keep one small connection between the current topic and a future system. If you are studying percentages, examine a fictional mass allocation. If you are studying functions, graph a sensor model. Then return to the complete foundational sequence rather than collecting only dramatic examples.',
        'Revisit your direction when you gain real information: a class, a project, a conversation with an engineer, or experience collaborating. Changing a specialization later can reflect better knowledge. The early work remains useful even if the eventual path includes mechanical, electrical, software, or ocean engineering.']
    ],
    'Write two statements: a long-term possibility you find compelling, and a capability you can demonstrate this month. Example: “space robotics” and “I can explain signed components on a coordinate grid.” Keep the second one testable.',
    'A flexible long-term goal can support a very concrete next skill.',[R.curriculum,R.astronauts,R.astronautAdvice]),
  article('math-as-team-language','meaning','Clear mathematics makes you easier to trust','An answer becomes useful when another person can understand it.',
    'An engineer rarely works alone with a perfect problem statement. People exchange measurements, assumptions, drawings, code, and decisions. Mathematics helps those people make their meaning precise. Learning to explain a small calculation is therefore part of learning to participate in technical work.',
    [
      ['A number needs a sentence',
        'Imagine handing a teammate a note that says “20.” It might mean seconds, kilograms, a percentage, or a maximum allowed value. Now write: “At a constant 3 m/s, the model needs 20 seconds to cover 60 meters.” The relationship, units, and assumption travel with the result.',
        'That sentence lets the teammate ask a useful question: does the speed really stay constant? Without the context, they might debate the arithmetic while missing the model. Good communication makes the important disagreement visible sooner.'],
      ['Explain the decision, not every pencil mark',
        'For a simple algebra solution, state the unknown, the equation, the crucial transformation, and the check. You do not need a paragraph defending 2 + 2. You do need to explain why a negative solution was rejected if the variable represents a physical length.',
        'As problems grow, the same structure can support a short technical note: question, inputs, assumptions, method, result, limitation. This is an editorial template you can practice now. It is not a substitute for the documentation standards of a future employer or laboratory.'],
      ['Practice both speaking and listening',
        'Explain a solved problem to a study partner without pointing at the answer key. Ask them to repeat what they believe your variable means. If their interpretation differs, revise the explanation rather than treating the misunderstanding as a personal failure.',
        'NASA’s spaceflight education material highlights communication, leadership, followership, and team care. These abilities develop through ordinary interactions as well as technical accomplishments. Listening carefully to a correction and making your work inspectable are practical contributions available long before an advanced degree.']
    ],
    'Turn “E = 48” into a complete explanation for a hypothetical 16-watt device used for 3 hours. Include the unit, constant-load assumption, and one limitation. Ask whether someone could reconstruct the calculation from your sentence.',
    'Clarity is a technical skill: it lets other people check, use, and improve your work.',[R.teamwork,R.systems]),
  article('read-with-a-pencil','learning','Read mathematics in small, active pieces','A useful reading session produces something you can do.',
    'Thirty minutes of reading followed by thirty minutes of problems can be a reasonable starting arrangement, but it is not a scientific optimum. In mathematics, the boundary should stay flexible. A single paragraph may deserve several minutes of drawing, calculation, and questioning before you read further.',
    [
      ['Choose a question before opening the section',
        'Instead of “read five pages,” choose “find out why multiplying both sides by the same nonzero number preserves an equation.” Preview the section title and one example. This gives the reading a job and makes it easier to notice which sentence answers your question.',
        'Cornell’s reading guidance encourages purpose, preview, and a plan for using the material. For a math book, turn that into a pencil task: define a symbol, fill in an omitted step, or create a small example that uses the idea.'],
      ['Interrupt the explanation productively',
        'Suppose the book moves from 4x − 7 = 9 to 4x = 16. Cover the next line and write what operation was applied. Then predict the next step before revealing it. If you cannot explain the transition, inspect that transition rather than rereading the entire page.',
        'Record only useful notes: a definition in your words, one representative example, a condition such as “denominator cannot be zero,” and an unresolved question. Copying every displayed equation can create a long record while leaving the central decision unexamined.'],
      ['End with a small transfer',
        'Close the book and solve 5x − 8 = 12. The numbers changed, so you must apply the relationship rather than remember the visible answer. Check x = 4 by substitution. If it fails, return to the exact step you need.',
        'Your reading pace will vary. A dense half-page can be a productive session, while ten easy pages may require little work. Keep reading connected to action and explanation; do not use page count as the main measure of whether you learned something.']
    ],
    'Read one worked equation example. Write three annotations: what each symbol means, why one transition is valid, and a similar problem. Solve the similar problem with the page closed.',
    'Read until there is an idea to test, then test it.',[R.reading,R.active]),
  article('worked-example-ladder','learning','Use a worked example as a ladder','Study the decisions, then gradually remove the support.',
    'When a topic is new, a complete solution can show you what productive work looks like. The danger is confusing familiarity with independence. Use the example to examine decisions, then reduce the help until you can make those decisions yourself on a related problem.',
    [
      ['Inspect the purpose of each step',
        'Consider 2(x + 3) = 14. One method first divides both sides by 2, giving x + 3 = 7, then subtracts 3. Another expands first. Both can work. Ask why a solver might prefer the shorter route instead of memorizing only the line sequence.',
        'Mark the moment where the method is selected. Is there a grouped expression that can be isolated? Is there a shared factor? That choice is often the part a novice needs to learn, even when every individual arithmetic operation is familiar.'],
      ['Remove one layer of support',
        'Start by covering the final answer and completing it. Next, cover the last two steps. Then solve a changed example such as 3(x + 2) = 18 without looking. This gradual removal is a practical study routine, not a requirement to use exactly three levels.',
        'If you get stuck, uncover only the next relevant step. Explain it before continuing. The aim is to regain direction while preserving some decision-making work, rather than copy the remaining solution to reach the appearance of completion.'],
      ['Test the structure, not the memory',
        'IES recommends alternating worked solutions with problems to solve. You can apply that principle by following one inspected example with a nearby problem, then returning later to a version that looks different. Keep the initial difficulty close enough that comparison is meaningful.',
        'Finally compare 2(x + 3) = 14 with 2x + 3 = 14. The placement of parentheses changes the structure. If you apply the same first step without noticing that difference, the example has not yet taught the decision you need. Slow down at the choice point.']
    ],
    'Solve 4(x − 1) = 20 in two ways: divide first and expand first. Both give x = 6. Explain which path uses fewer steps and why neither path is automatically required.',
    'A worked solution is useful when it helps you make the next decision yourself.',[R.ies]),
  article('retrieval-not-recognition','learning','Find out what you can recall without the page','Recognition is comfortable; retrieval gives you better evidence.',
    'A solution can look completely obvious while you are reading it and become difficult as soon as the page closes. That gap is useful information. A short attempt from memory tests whether the idea is available when you need it, without the visual cues doing part of the work.',
    [
      ['Start with something small',
        'After studying equivalent fractions, close the resource and answer: “What does it mean to multiply a fraction by 1?” Then produce an example such as 2/3 = 8/12. You are retrieving a reason and an operation, not merely reciting a definition.',
        'A blank page can hold a diagram, an equation, a sentence, or a worked problem. Choose the format that fits the objective. Remembering the word “denominator” is different from being able to use common denominators in an unfamiliar addition problem.'],
      ['Check promptly and repair precisely',
        'Open the source after making a genuine attempt. Compare what you produced with the explanation. If you wrote 2/3 = 4/9, identify that the numerator and denominator were multiplied by different factors. Then create and solve a new example correctly.',
        'Do not turn retrieval into an extended guessing ritual when you lack the prerequisite. A short unsuccessful attempt can reveal what needs instruction. After you receive that instruction, try again. The feedback is part of the learning process.'],
      ['Keep the stakes low',
        'IES supports using quizzes to revisit key content, and Cornell describes recall from a blank page as a study strategy. Neither requires treating every attempt as a judgment of intelligence. You can use a two-question check privately and record the specific result.',
        'Over time, compare performance after a delay with performance immediately after reading. If you can recognize an answer but cannot reconstruct it, you have found a target for review. If you can explain and apply it in a new setting, you have stronger evidence than familiarity alone provides.']
    ],
    'Without notes, explain why 0.25 equals 1/4 and calculate 0.25 × 28. Check: 7. Then use a different number to test whether you can repeat the reasoning rather than remember the result.',
    'A small closed-book attempt reveals the next useful learning action.',[R.ies,R.study]),
  article('spacing-without-a-perfect-calendar','learning','Return to an idea after it has cooled','You do not need a complicated schedule to use spaced practice.',
    'Solving a topic repeatedly in one sitting can make it feel secure. Returning later asks a different question: can you still find and use the method after attention has moved elsewhere? That is why a small amount of older material deserves a place beside the current topic.',
    [
      ['Make the return easy to arrange',
        'Keep a short list of ideas worth revisiting, with one representative question for each. “Subtracting a negative” is more useful than “Chapter 2.” After a study session, choose one item to revisit on a later day and another to revisit after a longer gap.',
        'The exact interval depends on your course, the topic, and how well you remember it. A next-day check and another later in the week are examples, not a universally optimal schedule. The essential feature is a meaningful gap followed by another attempt.'],
      ['Use the return as a diagnostic',
        'Suppose you learned 7 − (−2) = 9. On a later day, try −3 − (−8). If you obtain 5 and can explain the direction change, move on. If the sign rule disappears, inspect a number-line example and then try another question.',
        'Make the review short enough to preserve time for new work. You do not need to redo every old exercise or restart the entire course after one forgotten step. Choose the smallest review that repairs the particular gap.'],
      ['Let difficulty inform the next interval',
        'The IES guide recommends distributing learning over time. In your own practice, a successful delayed attempt can justify a longer gap, while an uncertain attempt can justify an earlier return. This is a practical adjustment rule, not a validated personal memory algorithm.',
        'A forgotten fact does not erase the earlier work. Treat the return as another learning opportunity. Record what changed: perhaps you remembered the procedure but forgot a condition, or could solve the equation but could not explain it. Those observations make the next review more specific.']
    ],
    'Choose three previously studied skills. Write one short question for each and set them aside. On a later day, attempt them before reopening the explanations; use the result to decide what needs a brief repair.',
    'Keep a small bridge to older material while moving forward.',[R.ies,R.study]),
  article('mix-after-a-foothold','learning','Mix problems when you have a foothold','Choosing a method is a skill of its own.',
    'A page titled “Multiply Fractions” tells you which operation to use before you read a question. A mixed page removes that clue. Both formats have a role: focused practice introduces a method, while mixed practice asks you to recognize when that method is appropriate.',
    [
      ['Build an initial route',
        'When fraction addition is brand new, a few closely related examples can help you see the common-denominator structure. Mixing in several unfamiliar procedures immediately may leave you guessing at all of them. Establish a usable starting method before demanding frequent method changes.',
        'This does not require flawless speed. A foothold means you can usually complete a straightforward example and explain the central decision. If not, use a worked example or targeted explanation before adding another layer of selection difficulty.'],
      ['Mix nearby distinctions',
        'Try 1/2 + 1/3, (1/2)(1/3), and (1/2) ÷ (1/3). Before calculating, say what each operation asks. Addition combines amounts, multiplication takes a fraction of an amount, and division asks how many of one amount fit into another.',
        'Then calculate 5/6, 1/6, and 3/2. The numerical ingredients are similar, but the relationships differ. That contrast makes the exercise about selecting and interpreting a method, rather than only carrying out a preselected recipe.'],
      ['Read the evidence at the right scale',
        'IES has reviewed a randomized study of interleaved mathematics practice. That supports taking the method seriously, but one study does not establish a universal schedule for every adult learner or every subject. Use performance and explanation to judge whether a particular mix is useful.',
        'If you can solve isolated questions but not mixed ones, work on recognition: underline the operation, name the unknown, or state why a method applies before using it. If you cannot solve either format, revisit instruction. More random difficulty is not automatically better practice.']
    ],
    'Create a six-question set using two skills you have already studied. Shuffle the order and label the method before solving each question. Review method-selection errors separately from arithmetic errors.',
    'Focused practice develops a tool; mixed practice develops the choice of tool.',[R.interleave,R.study]),
  article('connect-representations','learning','Use words, pictures, and equations together','Understanding grows when you can translate between descriptions.',
    'A concept can appear as a sentence, a diagram, a table, a graph, or an equation. Each representation makes some features easier to see. Learning to connect them helps prevent a familiar symbol from becoming a procedure whose meaning you have lost.',
    [
      ['Give the representations one shared story',
        'Imagine a tank containing 8 liters and receiving 2 liters each minute. In words, the amount starts at eight and rises steadily. A table gives 8, 10, 12, and 14 liters at 0, 1, 2, and 3 minutes. An equation is V = 8 + 2t.',
        'A graph shows a line with an initial vertical value of 8 and a rise of 2 for each minute. These are not four unrelated facts to memorize. They are four views of the same constant-rate model, with liters and minutes attached.'],
      ['Use disagreement as a clue',
        'If your table starts at zero but the story says eight liters are already present, one representation is inconsistent. If the graph falls while the equation adds 2t, inspect the plotted points or axis direction. Translation creates useful opportunities to catch an error.',
        'Pictures need labels. An unlabeled rising line does not tell you whether you are viewing volume, speed, or temperature. Likewise, a diagram should clarify a relationship rather than decorate the page. A simple sketch can be enough.'],
      ['Choose the view that answers the question',
        'IES recommends connecting concrete and abstract representations and combining graphics with verbal explanations. In practice, begin with the form that makes the question understandable, then connect it to the notation you need to use.',
        'For the tank, a table makes the first few values easy to inspect; the equation makes the 20-minute value easy to calculate. A graph can show when a capacity is reached, provided you respect the model’s range. Translating among them develops flexibility without requiring a new trick for every presentation.']
    ],
    'For a hypothetical object moving at 3 meters per second from position 5 meters, create a sentence, a four-row table, and an equation. Check: x = 5 + 3t. Name the assumptions and axis units.',
    'A representation is useful when it preserves meaning and reveals something you need.',[R.ies,R.functions]),
  article('choose-the-next-problem','practice','Choose problems that answer a learning question','More exercises help most when you know what they are testing.',
    'You do not need to complete every exercise in every book. You do need enough varied evidence to tell whether a method is understood, usable, and remembered. Choose the next question according to the uncertainty you want to resolve about your current skill.',
    [
      ['Begin with a representative example',
        'If you are learning percent, a straightforward question such as “What is 15% of 80?” checks whether you can interpret a percentage as a multiplier. The answer is 12. Write 0.15 × 80 so the relationship remains visible rather than relying on an unexplained mental shortcut.',
        'Then vary the task: “12 is what percent of 80?” now asks for the multiplier. The same numbers support a different unknown. If you can do the first but not the second, you have found a specific limit in your current understanding.'],
      ['Vary one feature deliberately',
        'Change the numbers, the unknown, the wording, or the representation one at a time. For example, compare a 15% discount with a 15% increase. Ask which amount the percentage acts on. The goal is to learn what stays the same and what changes.',
        'A challenge problem can be valuable, but it should not be your only evidence. Several hidden prerequisites may explain why it feels impossible. Use simpler questions to separate those prerequisites, then return to the larger problem with a clearer diagnosis.'],
      ['Know why you are stopping',
        'Stop a focused set when it has answered its learning question, then schedule a later check or move to a useful variation. If you are still guessing, a worked explanation may be more valuable than ten additional guesses. If everything is automatic, method selection or application may be the next challenge.',
        'Cornell’s exam-review guidance encourages examining errors and changing conditions in practice questions. The selection routine here is an original way to apply that idea. It is not a universal number of problems or a rule that a particular score proves mastery.']
    ],
    'Build a four-question percent set: find a part, find a percent, find the original whole, and compare an increase with a decrease. Explain which unknown changes across the set before solving it.',
    'Pick the next problem for the information it will give you.',[R.feedback]),
  article('an-error-log-that-changes-work','practice','Keep an error log that changes your next attempt','A useful note explains the first broken step.',
    'An error log should be a small repair tool, not a museum of everything you have ever done wrong. Record recurring or revealing mistakes, explain their cause as precisely as you can, and connect each entry to a fresh problem that tests the repair.',
    [
      ['Describe the event without labeling yourself',
        'Instead of “I am careless with fractions,” write: “I added 1/2 and 1/3 as 2/5 because I treated denominators like counts.” The second statement identifies an operation and an interpretation. It gives you something concrete to correct.',
        'Now write the repair: denominators name piece sizes, so rewrite the amounts in sixths before combining them. The correct result is 5/6. Add a quick reasonableness check: the sum must exceed 1/2, while 2/5 does not.'],
      ['Separate different kinds of error',
        'A useful distinction is between misunderstanding the idea, choosing the wrong method, making an execution slip, and misreading the question. These are working categories, not permanent traits. A single problem can contain more than one type.',
        'If the issue was an execution slip, a cleaner layout or a sign check may help. If the method was wrong, more neatly repeating it will not. If the wording was misunderstood, practice translating quantities and identifying the requested unknown before computing.'],
      ['Close the loop',
        'Choose a new example, such as 3/4 + 1/6, and solve it without copying the correction. Check: 11/12. Revisit it later if the error was persistent. The log has served its purpose when it changes what you do, not when it becomes impressively long.',
        'Cornell recommends learning from returned assessments by asking why an answer failed and what would improve the next attempt. Keep the factual claim modest: reviewing feedback is useful; your personal log format is a practical experiment. Revise it if writing notes consumes time without improving subsequent work.']
    ],
    'Create one entry with four parts: the incorrect step, the reason it failed, a corrected explanation, and a new solved example. Mark whether the new attempt needed help; that distinction makes the record more informative.',
    'An error becomes valuable when its explanation changes a future action.',[R.feedback]),
  article('a-protocol-for-being-stuck','practice','When you are stuck, change the question you ask','Move from “I cannot do this” to a smaller, answerable problem.',
    'Being stuck can mean several things: you do not understand the words, lack a prerequisite, cannot choose a method, or made a mistake midway. A repeatable investigation helps you tell those situations apart before you decide whether to keep working or seek help.',
    [
      ['Locate the uncertainty',
        'First restate the question in plain language and name the unknown with its unit. For “A 12-liter tank fills at 3 liters per minute; how long from empty?” the unknown is time in minutes. If you cannot identify that, calculation is premature.',
        'List what is given and sketch the relationship. A short table—3 liters after one minute, 6 after two—may reveal that four minutes reaches twelve. The picture or table is a tool for finding structure, not a fallback reserved for people who are “bad at algebra.”'],
      ['Reduce the problem without abandoning it',
        'Try easier numbers, a special case, or a related example whose method you understand. If a complicated fraction obscures a percent relationship, temporarily use 100 as the whole. Then return to the actual numbers and check that your method still applies.',
        'Inspect the last step you can justify. Say exactly why the next step is uncertain. A question such as “Why can I divide both sides by this expression when it might be zero?” is ready for a textbook, tutor, or instructor to address.'],
      ['Use help with a clear request',
        'After a reasonable attempt that is no longer producing new information, ask for a hint or an explanation of the missing step. There is no universal number of minutes that separates productive effort from wasted effort. The important signal is whether your investigation is changing.',
        'Cornell’s office-hours guidance recommends bringing attempted work and specific questions. After receiving help, solve a nearby problem independently. Otherwise you may leave with a clear memory of the tutor’s solution but no evidence that you can make the same decisions yourself.']
    ],
    'Choose one problem that stalled you. Write: what is asked, what is given, the last step you trust, and one precise question. If you cannot solve it yet, that written diagnosis is still a useful deliverable.',
    'A good stuck protocol produces either a next step or a precise request for help.',[R.office,R.active]),
  article('check-in-another-way','practice','Check an answer with a different kind of evidence','Repeating the same calculation can repeat the same mistake.',
    'An answer key tells you whether your final result matches one expected result. It does not always tell you whether the reasoning was valid. Develop a small set of independent checks so you can investigate your own work before relying on an external verdict.',
    [
      ['Use the original statement',
        'If you solve 5x + 4 = 29 and obtain x = 5, substitute it into the original equation: 5(5) + 4 = 29. Checking an intermediate equation is weaker if the mistake happened while producing that equation.',
        'For a word problem, check the meaning too. A calculated count of 3.7 identical containers may be mathematically valid in a continuous model but unsuitable when the question asks how many whole containers can be packed. The context supplies an additional constraint.'],
      ['Check scale, units, and direction',
        'For 0.4 × 30, expect a positive answer smaller than 30. For a speed of 60 km/h converted to m/s, expect a value much smaller than 60. For a displacement described as westward when east is positive, expect a negative horizontal component.',
        'These checks do not prove every digit, but they catch different classes of error. Unit agreement is necessary for a physical equation and still does not establish that the model itself is correct. Keep each check’s strength and limitation clear.'],
      ['Choose one check you did not already use',
        'If you solved by algebra, try a graph or substitution. If you solved by a table, derive the equation. If a calculator produced a result, estimate it with rounded numbers. Two methods are most informative when they do not merely repeat the same hidden assumption.',
        'NASA distinguishes verification of specified requirements from validation of intended use. Your homework checks are much smaller, but the analogy is useful: check the calculation and ask whether the calculation answers the actual question. Neither question replaces the other.']
    ],
    'Solve 3x − 8 = 13. Check x = 7 by substitution, then explain why x must be greater than 13/3 before solving. Identify which check confirms equality and which only checks plausibility.',
    'Use a second route that could fail differently from the first.',[R.systems,R.feedback]),
  article('tutor-and-ai-without-outsourcing','practice','Use a tutor or AI to improve your next attempt','Help is successful when you can do more after it disappears.',
    'A tutor, instructor, solution manual, or AI tool can make a confusing step understandable. It can also perform the thinking you intended to practice. Decide which role the help should play before asking, and preserve an independent attempt afterward.',
    [
      ['Ask for the smallest useful help',
        'Show your work and identify the uncertainty. For example: “I expanded −2(x − 5) as −2x − 10. Point to the first incorrect operation and give me one question to think about; do not finish the problem.” That request directs attention to a decision.',
        'A human tutor can respond to your explanation and ask follow-up questions. An AI system may generate a fluent but incorrect explanation, so compare important claims with a trusted text or instructor and check the arithmetic yourself. Confidence of presentation is not evidence of correctness.'],
      ['Make a clean attempt afterward',
        'Once the sign issue is explained, close the assistance and solve −3(y − 4). You should obtain −3y + 12. Explain the two products and test y = 0. That new attempt is the evidence that the help changed your understanding.',
        'If you can only solve while the assistant supplies each next line, ask for a simpler prerequisite exercise. You may need more instruction before a fully independent attempt is useful. There is no benefit in pretending the assisted performance was unaided.'],
      ['Keep the tool inside the learning goal',
        'Cornell’s AI guidance emphasizes keeping responsibility for learning and following course-specific rules. Use only assistance permitted for the assignment. For personal practice, you can ask for additional examples, alternative explanations, or questions that test your reasoning, while still checking their accuracy.',
        'Do not upload private classmate information or confidential project material simply to get a better explanation. Keep a note of which problems used hints or complete solutions. This is not a confession; it helps you choose an honest later test of what you can now do independently.']
    ],
    'Write a help request containing your attempt, the exact uncertain step, and the level of help desired. After receiving help, solve a changed problem without the conversation visible and verify it independently.',
    'Use assistance to build capability, and measure the capability after assistance is removed.',[R.ai,R.office]),
  article('readiness-with-evidence','practice','Decide readiness with several small pieces of evidence','One score should not carry the whole decision.',
    'A brief diagnostic can suggest where to begin, but it cannot certify mastery of an entire subject. A useful readiness decision combines ordinary problems, explanations, delayed recall, and the requirements of the actual next course. Treat a score as one clue within that larger picture.',
    [
      ['Distinguish what the result means',
        'A wrong answer may reflect a misunderstanding, a slip, or unfamiliar wording. A skipped question means you did not provide an answer. “Not learned yet” identifies missing instruction. Those outcomes should lead to different next actions instead of being collapsed into a story about your ability.',
        'If a short quiz samples only two fraction questions, success is encouraging but does not cover every operation with fractions. Look at the sampled skills and the missing ones. Small assessments are most useful when their limits are visible.'],
      ['Use a miniature demonstration',
        'For linear equations, attempt several forms: x + 7 = 12, 3x = 18, 2x + 5 = 17, and 2(x + 1) = 10. Explain one solution, check another, and revisit one later. This gives more varied evidence than repeating one format twenty times.',
        'You can then ask whether the next topic requires something else, such as fractions or distribution. Readiness is relational: ready for which task, with which tools, and under which conditions? “Good at algebra” is too broad to guide a specific choice.'],
      ['Keep official placement separate',
        'For a college course, use the college’s current placement process and advising guidance. This website’s questions and suggested thresholds are informal editorial tools. They do not determine transfer credit, admission, or permission to enroll.',
        'If you are uncertain, bring worked examples to a tutor or instructor and ask what prerequisite gaps they see. The decision can be “begin the next topic while reviewing fractions,” rather than either “perfectly ready” or “start everything again.” A targeted bridge often makes more sense than a global judgment.']
    ],
    'Choose one upcoming topic. List three prerequisite skills, demonstrate each with a solved example, and mark whether the attempt was independent, hinted, or copied. Use the weakest prerequisite to choose a focused review.',
    'Readiness is a claim about a specific next task, supported by specific evidence.',[R.feedback,R.office]),
  article('a-load-you-can-sustain','resilience','Choose a workload you can return to','A plan should fit your actual life, not only your most energetic day.',
    'A demanding goal does not require every week to be maximally demanding. Work, family, classes, travel, and ordinary fatigue all affect the time available for learning. A useful plan accounts for those constraints so that progress can continue beyond the first burst of enthusiasm.',
    [
      ['Budget the whole commitment',
        'A class consumes more than the hours shown on its meeting schedule. Reading, homework, review, commuting, and getting help also require time. Before choosing one to three courses, examine the combined workload and current prerequisites with the college’s guidance.',
        'For self-study, notice the difference between reserving an hour and having an hour of usable attention. If a session consistently collapses halfway through, try a shorter focused block with a clear stopping point. The result matters more than preserving an impressive-looking number.'],
      ['Protect the conditions for good work',
        'Cornell’s study guidance includes rest and a realistic study plan. You do not need a medical theory or an exact universal sleep prescription to avoid making all-night study the default. Treat sleep, meals, breaks, and existing responsibilities as parts of the schedule rather than leftovers.',
        'If a late session becomes repeated rereading without understanding, write the next question and stop at a clean boundary. Returning with a specific task is usually easier than returning to a pile of unfinished, unexplained marks. Adjust the workload instead of interpreting every tired evening as lost commitment.'],
      ['Review capacity using evidence',
        'Track whether the plan leaves room to complete assignments, repair mistakes, and revisit older material. If new work continually replaces review and help-seeking, the total load may be too high even when you are technically submitting everything.',
        'A tentative 2033 graduation horizon should be checked against real course sequencing as your enrollment develops. It should not force you to conceal gaps now. Strong grades and durable prerequisites may justify a different pace than an initial estimate; the purpose is to support later learning rather than win a race against a draft calendar.']
    ],
    'Estimate the weekly time required by one proposed class, including study and travel. Compare it with genuinely available time after fixed commitments. Identify one adjustment that creates room for review or tutoring.',
    'A sustainable plan includes the conditions that let you learn, not just hours assigned to work.',[R.time,R.study]),
  article('make-starting-smaller','resilience','Make the first action smaller than the whole goal','Starting arithmetic does not require solving your entire future.',
    '“Become ready for aerospace engineering” is a meaningful direction and an enormous immediate task. When it is time to study, replace that broad instruction with an action small enough to begin without negotiating with the whole future. The destination can stay large while the opening move stays concrete.',
    [
      ['Specify an action you can see',
        '“Study fractions” leaves many choices unresolved. “Draw two equal rectangles and show 1/2 and 2/4” tells you what to do first. After that, you can explain why the shaded amounts match and solve a related example.',
        'Prepare the material before the session: one page, a pencil, and the question you intend to answer. Reducing setup decisions can make it easier to begin. This is a practical organization suggestion, not a claim that a particular workspace or timer is necessary for success.'],
      ['Use a short beginning without making it a trap',
        'Cornell suggests breaking large tasks into manageable pieces and using a small timed start when inertia is the problem. You can try a few minutes of one clear activity, then decide whether to continue. The short start should remain a legitimate action, not a disguised promise to work for hours.',
        'If you still cannot begin, inspect what is unclear. Perhaps the resource starts above your level, the task is too vague, or you are avoiding a repeated difficulty. Make the obstacle specific enough that you can change the task or ask for help.'],
      ['Leave a useful next edge',
        'At the end, write one sentence describing the next action: “Try 3/4 + 1/8 without the example.” That sentence is more useful than “continue chapter.” It gives the next session a low-friction entry point and preserves the context of today’s work.',
        'Small starts are not a substitute for enough total study. Their job is to help you enter useful work consistently. Over time, judge whether they lead to completed learning cycles: attempt, feedback, repair, and later return. If they only produce repeated setup, change the pattern.']
    ],
    'Rewrite one vague study intention as a visible action that takes only a few minutes to begin. Complete it, then write the exact next question before deciding whether to continue.',
    'Shrink the opening action while keeping the larger direction intact.',[R.start]),
  article('diagnose-boredom','resilience','Treat boredom as information','The next change depends on why the work feels flat.',
    'Boredom is not one single signal. It may mean you already know the procedure, cannot see its purpose, are working above your understanding, or have spent too long watching someone else think. Before buying another course, test which explanation best fits the current session.',
    [
      ['Check whether the skill is actually easy',
        'Close the explanation and solve a fresh problem. If you can do it accurately, explain why, and handle a small variation, you may be ready for mixed practice or the next topic. If the page looked easy but the attempt fails, the issue may be recognition without independence.',
        'For example, watching someone calculate a percentage can feel repetitive. Asking you to recover the original price after a discount changes the task. A 20% discount leaves 80% of the original; if the new price is 64, the original was 80.'],
      ['Change one part of the activity',
        'If the procedure is familiar, add a boundary question: when would it not apply? If the purpose is missing, connect it to a small model. If the explanation is too dense, choose a simpler worked example. If attention has faded, stop and return later.',
        'Avoid changing the whole learning system every time a session feels ordinary. A different font, app, or instructor may help, but constant switching also requires you to learn a new structure. First identify the specific gap the new resource is supposed to fill.'],
      ['Allow useful work to be unglamorous',
        'Some practice remains worth doing even when it is not entertaining. You can agree to a bounded amount because it supports a capability you value. The important distinction is between purposeful repetition and repetition that no longer tells you anything new.',
        'The framework here is editorial advice, informed by active-study and task-management guidance. It is not a diagnostic test for motivation. Try a small change, observe whether it improves the next independent attempt, and keep the change only if it serves learning.']
    ],
    'Rate one task as too easy, unclear, too difficult, or simply tiring. Choose a matching adjustment and attempt one new problem. Compare the result with the original situation before changing resources.',
    'Ask what boredom is pointing to before deciding what it means about you.',[R.active,R.start]),
  article('measure-progress-fairly','resilience','Notice progress that a chapter counter misses','A more independent solution can matter more than another completed page.',
    'Progress through foundations is not always visible as constant forward movement. You may spend time repairing fractions while studying equations, or return to graph reading after learning a formula. A fair record tracks changes in capability, not only the number of topics you have visited.',
    [
      ['Compare like with like',
        'If last week you solved an equation with a complete example visible and this week solved a similar one with one hint, that is a change worth recording. It is not yet full independence, but it tells you the support requirement has decreased.',
        'Keep a few representative dated attempts. Look for clearer variable definitions, fewer unexplained jumps, better checking, or successful recall after a delay. Comparing today’s hardest unfamiliar problem with yesterday’s easiest familiar one gives a distorted picture.'],
      ['Separate a gap from a verdict',
        'Suppose distributing negatives remains difficult while solving positive-coefficient equations is comfortable. Write that narrow observation. It suggests a targeted repair. “I am not a math person” does not identify what to study and is much broader than the evidence supports.',
        'Equally, a successful easy set does not establish that all prerequisites are complete. Accurate self-assessment includes both strengths and limits. A useful record can say, “I can do this form independently; I still need help when fractions appear.”'],
      ['Use review to make progress visible',
        'Revisit an older question that once required substantial help. Explain the method before checking the answer. If it now feels simpler, note which decisions became easier. If it does not, you have found a review target rather than proof that the entire effort was wasted.',
        'Cornell’s guidance on feedback and recall supports examining what you can actually produce. The progress measures here are practical examples, not a validated scale. Avoid inventing precision such as “I am 73% ready for engineering” from a small practice bank. Specific demonstrations are more honest and more useful.']
    ],
    'Choose two dated attempts at the same kind of problem. Identify one improved decision and one remaining difficulty. Write the next exercise that would test the difficulty directly, without restarting unrelated topics.',
    'Measure what became more accurate, explainable, durable, or independent.',[R.feedback,R.study]),
  article('learn-with-people','resilience','Use other people before the gap becomes a crisis','Questions and collaboration are normal parts of technical learning.',
    'Self-study does not have to mean solitary study. A tutor, instructor, classmate, or study group can help expose a misunderstanding you have been repeating. The goal is not to have someone carry the work, but to create a conversation that improves your own reasoning.',
    [
      ['Bring something discussable',
        'Prepare a problem, your attempt, and one question. For example: “I can solve 2x + 5 = 13, but I do not know why dividing first gives fractions. Is that method invalid or just less convenient?” This gives a helper more to work with than “I do not understand algebra.”',
        'You do not need a polished attempt before asking. Even identifying where the wording becomes unclear is useful. If you have no foothold at all, say which prerequisite you last understood and ask for a smaller starting example.'],
      ['Make collaboration require participation',
        'In a study pair, solve a short question separately before comparing. One person explains a method; the other asks why a step is valid or proposes a check. Then switch roles. This avoids turning the session into one person performing while the other watches.',
        'Cornell’s group-study guidance emphasizes expectations and shared participation. Follow the actual course rules about collaboration. Personal practice can be flexible, while graded assignments may restrict what students can work on together or what assistance must be acknowledged.'],
      ['Return to individual evidence',
        'After a helpful conversation, solve a changed question alone. Write what the discussion clarified and what still feels uncertain. This lets you tell the difference between understanding an explanation in the moment and being able to use the idea independently.',
        'For your future engineering work, asking precise questions and responding well to correction are valuable habits. They also make the present learning experience more workable. Seeking help early is compatible with ambition; it can prevent a small missing idea from disrupting several later topics.']
    ],
    'Prepare a two-minute explanation of one solved problem and one question about it. In a study meeting, ask someone to challenge a step. Afterward, solve a related problem independently and record the difference.',
    'Good collaboration increases what each person can understand and do afterward.',[R.office,R.peers]),
  article('return-after-a-gap','resilience','Return after a gap without restarting your identity','Resume from evidence, not from punishment.',
    'A missed week or a longer interruption does not tell you exactly how much mathematics you remember. It tells you that there was an interruption. Begin again with a small sample of prior work, use it to locate the current starting point, and rebuild continuity from there.',
    [
      ['Sample before restarting',
        'Choose a few representative questions from the last comfortable topic and one earlier prerequisite. Attempt them without notes. If most reasoning returns, continue with a brief review. If a particular step is missing, review that step instead of automatically returning to page one.',
        'For example, you might still solve 3x + 2 = 14 but forget how to combine 1/3 and 1/4. That suggests fraction review alongside equations. It does not imply that every arithmetic skill has disappeared or that the equation work was meaningless.'],
      ['Rebuild a manageable loop',
        'Use one main resource, one short problem set, and a visible next action. Reopening six unfinished courses can make the return feel like an inventory of obligations. Select the resource that best fits the current gap and let the others wait.',
        'The first session can end after a complete learning cycle: attempt, inspect feedback, correct a misunderstanding, and solve a fresh example. That gives you a concrete piece of completed work rather than a promise to compensate for every missed hour.'],
      ['Adjust the plan that failed',
        'Ask what interrupted the previous pattern: unavailable time, unclear tasks, excessive difficulty, resource overload, or another responsibility. Choose an adjustment related to that cause. A more demanding version of the same plan is not automatically a better response.',
        'Keep the long-term space or engineering interest as context while letting today’s evidence determine the next mathematical step. A tentative graduation date may need later revision; that is a planning question, not a measure of personal worth. You can pursue a serious goal through an imperfect sequence of ordinary returns.']
    ],
    'Make a five-question restart sample using previously studied skills. Sort the results into comfortable, needs a brief review, and needs instruction. Choose one repair and one next-session task, rather than making a new complete curriculum.',
    'A return becomes productive when it identifies the next workable step.',[R.start,R.time])
];

export const engineeringHabits = [
  article('carry-the-units','engineering','Carry the units through the calculation','Treat the label as part of the quantity.',
    'A measured quantity includes a numerical value and a unit. Keeping both visible gives you another line of reasoning beside the arithmetic. This habit is useful immediately, whether you are calculating a drawing scale or reading a future spacecraft specification.',
    [
      ['Make conversion a relationship',
        'Convert 450 millimeters to meters by multiplying 450 mm × 1 m/1,000 mm = 0.450 m. The conversion factor equals one physical length divided by that same length. It changes the description while preserving the quantity.',
        'Write the units so the cancellation is visible. If you accidentally use 1,000 mm/1 m, the unwanted millimeters do not cancel in the intended way. That is a warning before you inspect the final number.'],
      ['Match dimensions as well as names',
        'An area has two length factors. Therefore 1 m² = (100 cm)² = 10,000 cm², not 100 cm². A volume conversion requires three factors. This is why memorizing only a direction to move a decimal point becomes fragile.',
        'Dimensional agreement can reject some bad equations, but it cannot prove a physical model is correct. Both 2vt and vt have dimensions of distance; only additional reasoning tells you which expression fits a particular situation.'],
      ['Make interfaces explicit',
        'NASA’s Mars Climate Orbiter investigation documents the consequences of incompatible unit expectations. Your beginner version is an input table with a unit column and an output statement that retains the unit.',
        'When handing a calculation to another person or program, state the required unit explicitly. “Time in seconds” and “angle in radians” are more useful than assuming everyone will infer the convention from context.']
    ],
    'Convert 2,500 cm² to m² using written unit factors. Check: 0.25 m². Draw a 50 cm by 50 cm square as a separate check.',
    'A bare number is usually an incomplete engineering input.',[R.units,R.geometry]),
  article('estimate-before-detail','engineering','Estimate before trusting the precise result','A rough answer creates a reference point.',
    'An estimate is a deliberately simplified calculation used to understand scale. It does not replace a careful answer. It gives you something independent to compare with that answer, especially when software displays many digits that can make a mistake look authoritative.',
    [
      ['Choose simple nearby numbers',
        'Suppose a fictional instrument draws 19.6 watts for 3.1 hours. Round to 20 watts and 3 hours to predict about 60 watt-hours. The exact product of the stated values is 60.76 Wh. The agreement in scale is reassuring.',
        'A software result of 607.6 Wh would deserve investigation before being placed in a report. A result of 60.7 Wh might reflect rounding rather than a large conceptual problem. The estimate helps distinguish the kinds of questions to ask.'],
      ['Know what the estimate does not show',
        'Rounding can hide a small error or make two different methods look equally plausible. An estimate near 60 does not establish the hundredths digit. It also does not establish whether the instrument actually draws a constant load.',
        'Use different levels of detail for different decisions. If two concepts differ by a factor of ten, a rough comparison may reveal an obvious tradeoff. If a requirement is nearly reached, you need a more careful model and appropriate uncertainty information.'],
      ['Write the estimate before the calculation',
        'Doing it first reduces the temptation to adjust the rough answer to match what you already saw. State the rounded inputs and why they are reasonable. A short note is enough for an early exercise.',
        'NASA’s design process includes analysis and comparison of alternatives. The estimate-first routine here is an original beginner application of that broader engineering practice, not a formal NASA acceptance method.']
    ],
    'Estimate 48.7 × 0.21 before calculating. A rough answer is about 10; the product is 10.227. Explain what errors the estimate could catch and what it could miss.',
    'Precision is easier to judge when you already understand the scale.',[R.design]),
  article('choose-positive-direction','engineering','Choose a positive direction before using signs','A minus sign needs a reference.',
    'In a physical model, a negative value often expresses direction or a difference relative to a reference. It does not automatically mean the result is impossible. Define the reference before calculating so that signs communicate a consistent meaning throughout the solution.',
    [
      ['Name the coordinate convention',
        'For a paper rover model, let east be positive x and north be positive y. A displacement of 4 meters west is then −4 meters in x. Starting at x = 7 meters and moving west 4 meters gives x = 3 meters.',
        'If another person chooses west as positive, their coordinates may have different signs while describing the same motion. Comparing bare numbers without comparing conventions can create a disagreement that is entirely about representation.'],
      ['Distinguish position, velocity, and acceleration',
        'A negative position means an object is on the negative side of the origin. A negative velocity means its position is decreasing. Neither statement alone determines the sign of acceleration, which describes how velocity changes.',
        'For a simple example, velocity changing from −6 m/s to −2 m/s over 2 seconds has average acceleration (+4 m/s)/2 s = +2 m/s². The object is moving in the negative direction while slowing down.'],
      ['Check the meaning before changing the sign',
        'Do not remove a minus sign merely because a final answer looks uncomfortable. Ask whether it follows from the chosen direction and whether the variable can be signed. A physical mass in an ordinary model has a different interpretation from a coordinate component.',
        'NASA’s introductory mechanics material treats forces and acceleration as vectors. You can prepare for that work now by drawing arrows and stating the axis convention beside every signed example.']
    ],
    'Choose upward as positive. A lift moves from y = 5 m to y = −2 m in 7 seconds. Find displacement and average velocity. Check: −7 m and −1 m/s; total distance needs the path.',
    'A sign is useful information when its reference is explicit.',[R.motion]),
  article('state-model-assumptions','engineering','Write the assumptions that make the equation usable','Every simple model leaves something out.',
    'A model is a selected description of a situation. Its usefulness depends on both the mathematics and the conditions under which you apply it. Naming those conditions lets another person decide whether your result answers the question they actually care about.',
    [
      ['Attach a condition to the formula',
        'The equation E = Pt calculates energy from a constant power and a duration. For a hypothetical 30-watt load operating for 2 hours, it gives 60 Wh. The constant-load condition is part of the model, even if the algebra is only one multiplication.',
        'If the load operates at 30 watts for one hour and 10 watts for another, the energy is 30 + 10 = 40 Wh. The original 60 Wh answer is not an arithmetic error; it describes a different assumed operation.'],
      ['Choose the boundary of the calculation',
        'Does “load” include only the instrument, or also communication electronics and conversion losses? Does “energy available” mean stored energy or energy delivered usefully? These questions determine which quantities belong in the equation.',
        'A simple teaching model can omit those details as long as it says so. A real design decision requires a boundary suited to that decision. More equations do not automatically repair an ambiguous boundary.'],
      ['Revisit assumptions when the question changes',
        'NASA’s systems-engineering material connects models and design choices to defined requirements and expectations. At beginner scale, keep an assumptions line beside your result and update it when you change the scenario.',
        'If you are comparing two fictional loads with identical operating conditions, a simple model may be enough. If you are selecting hardware, its operating limits and measured behavior become relevant. Match the model to the decision rather than using one familiar formula everywhere.']
    ],
    'Calculate energy for a 20-watt load used for 3 hours, then replace the final hour with a 5-watt standby mode. Check: 60 Wh versus 45 Wh. Identify the changed assumption.',
    'An equation becomes a model when its quantities, boundary, and conditions are defined.',[R.design,R.power]),
  article('test-boundaries-and-limits','engineering','Test the edges of the model','Special cases expose mistakes that ordinary inputs can hide.',
    'A formula can behave sensibly for one example and fail at a boundary. Checking zero, a sign change, an extreme value, or an excluded input helps you discover where the mathematics or the physical interpretation stops being appropriate.',
    [
      ['Identify forbidden inputs',
        'For t = d/v with a positive distance, v = 0 is not an ordinary input: division by zero is undefined. A negative v may also be outside a model that defines v as a positive constant speed toward the destination.',
        'Do not let a calculator’s error message be the first time you think about this restriction. Write the domain while setting up the problem. The appropriate restrictions can come from mathematics, from the scenario, or from both.'],
      ['Try a simple limiting case',
        'A tank model V = 10 + 2t should give 10 liters at t = 0. If your rearranged expression gives zero, something changed incorrectly. But if the tank holds only 30 liters, continuing the line past t = 10 minutes requires a new description of overflow or stopped filling.',
        'The line remains a mathematically valid expression outside that interval. What fails is the interpretation as the contained volume under the original physical assumptions. This distinction is central to using models responsibly.'],
      ['Use tests as questions, not proof of everything',
        'Passing several special cases does not establish that a complicated model is correct for every allowed input. It provides targeted evidence and often catches obvious implementation errors cheaply.',
        'NASA’s verification guidance uses planned evaluation against requirements. Your boundary tests are a small learning analogue: decide what an input should mean, predict an expected behavior, and compare the model with that expectation.']
    ],
    'For C = 4 + 3n, interpret C as total mass in kilograms for n identical modules. Check n = 0, n = 2, and n = −1. Which values are algebraically valid, and which fit a module count?',
    'Always ask where an expression is defined and where its interpretation is valid.',[R.verification,R.functions]),
  article('verification-and-validation','engineering','Check the result and check the question','Correctly solving the wrong problem is still a problem.',
    'NASA distinguishes verification against specified requirements from validation of intended use. You can practice the underlying distinction with ordinary mathematics: first check whether the calculation follows its stated rules, then ask whether those rules describe the need.',
    [
      ['Verify a small calculation',
        'Suppose a fictional storage model calculates capacity as number of boxes multiplied by space per box. Ten boxes at 2 liters each give 20 liters. You can verify the multiplication, the units, and whether the implementation reproduces simple known examples.',
        'If a spreadsheet returns 200 liters, the calculation or its inputs need investigation. This is a question about whether the implemented relation is being evaluated as specified.'],
      ['Validate the intended use',
        'Now suppose the user needs to pack ten fragile instruments, each requiring padding. A bare-volume sum may not answer whether the actual container is suitable. The model can be computed perfectly while omitting the feature that matters to the user.',
        'Validation asks about the purpose and operating situation. For this teaching example, you might need dimensions, clearances, and an agreed packing rule. The exact evidence depends on the real task; one multiplication cannot establish physical suitability.'],
      ['Keep both questions visible',
        'On a homework page, verification might include substitution and unit checks. Validation might include checking that the variable represents the requested quantity and that assumptions fit the story. These are educational analogies, not a claim that homework checks meet aerospace certification procedures.',
        'A useful final sentence states both what the calculation establishes and what it leaves unresolved. That makes the result easier to use without silently extending it beyond the evidence.']
    ],
    'A calculator correctly evaluates 100 ÷ 5 = 20. Invent one travel scenario where 20 seconds answers the question and one where it does not because the speed changes. Explain the distinction.',
    'Reliable work checks both compliance with the method and suitability for the purpose.',[R.systems,R.verification]),
  article('change-one-input','engineering','Change one input and watch the result','Sensitivity turns a formula into a design conversation.',
    'Sensitivity asks how an output responds when an input changes. You can explore it with arithmetic long before calculus. The exercise helps reveal which quantities deserve attention and prevents the assumption that every 10% input change produces a 10% output change.',
    [
      ['Start with a clear baseline',
        'For a hypothetical 120-meter journey at a constant 3 m/s, time is 120/3 = 40 seconds. Increase speed by 10% to 3.3 m/s while keeping distance fixed. The new time is about 36.36 seconds.',
        'Time fell by about 3.64 seconds, or 9.1% of the original 40 seconds. The output did not fall by exactly 10%, because speed appears in the denominator. Naming the baseline makes the percentage comparison unambiguous.'],
      ['Keep other inputs fixed',
        'If you change speed and distance together, you can still study the result, but you no longer isolate the effect of speed. For an introductory sensitivity table, change one input at a time and record which values remained fixed.',
        'Then compare two different relationships. Doubling a constant load doubles energy for the same duration. Doubling every side of a cube multiplies volume by eight. The mathematical structure determines the response.'],
      ['Use the result to ask better questions',
        'NASA’s technical-management guidance includes sensitivity analysis when examining requirements and changes. Our small table is a beginner illustration, not a full design trade study.',
        'A sensitive input may deserve better measurement or a stronger justification. It is not automatically the most important input overall: cost, feasibility, uncertainty, and consequences also matter. Sensitivity gives you one useful part of a broader decision.']
    ],
    'For a constant 20-watt load, compare durations of 2, 2.2, and 1.8 hours. Check energies of 40, 44, and 36 Wh. Explain why the percentages match here but not in the speed example.',
    'Changing an input deliberately reveals the structure of the relationship.',[R.management]),
  article('uncertainty-is-information','engineering','Report uncertainty without inventing precision','A measured value is not an unlimited supply of exact digits.',
    'A ruler reading, a sensor value, and an early mass estimate do not all have the same certainty. Keep the source and meaning of an uncertain input visible. For a beginner, the first useful step is distinguishing an exact exercise value from a measurement or an estimate.',
    [
      ['Begin with an explicit interval example',
        'Suppose, purely for practice, a rectangle’s length is bounded between 9.9 and 10.1 cm and its width between 4.9 and 5.1 cm. With positive dimensions, its area is bounded by 9.9 × 4.9 = 48.51 cm² and 10.1 × 5.1 = 51.51 cm².',
        'The central values give 50 cm². The interval communicates information that “50.000000” does not. These are assumed hard bounds for the exercise, not a statistically derived confidence interval.'],
      ['Keep uncertainty separate from a mistake',
        'A correctly calculated result can remain uncertain because its inputs are uncertain. An arithmetic error is a different issue. Repeating the same computation more carefully will not remove uncertainty in an unmeasured dimension.',
        'Likewise, more displayed decimal places do not make a sensor more accurate. Choose reporting precision that fits the information available, and keep enough internal precision to avoid unnecessary rounding error during calculations.'],
      ['Say how the uncertainty was obtained',
        'NIST’s uncertainty guidance emphasizes evaluating and reporting measurement uncertainty. Formal practice is more detailed than the interval exercise here. Do not attach a confidence percentage unless you have a method that justifies it.',
        'For now, state whether a range is specified, estimated, measured repeatedly, or chosen for a hypothetical scenario. That transparency helps another person decide how much weight the result can bear.']
    ],
    'Using assumed length bounds of 3.9–4.1 m and width bounds of 1.9–2.1 m, bound the rectangular area. Check: 7.41–8.61 m². Explain why this is not automatically a 95% confidence interval.',
    'Uncertainty describes the evidence behind a value; extra digits do not replace that evidence.',[R.uncertainty]),
  article('make-requirements-checkable','engineering','Turn a wish into a checkable requirement','A useful constraint says what would count as success.',
    '“Make it light and efficient” expresses a direction, but it does not specify how to evaluate a design. A requirement becomes more useful when the quantity, boundary, condition, and acceptance criterion are clear enough that two people can apply the same check.',
    [
      ['Replace an adjective with a condition',
        'For a fictional classroom model, replace “the package should be small” with “the assembled paper model must fit inside a 20 cm × 15 cm rectangle when viewed from above.” Now a drawing and measurements can address the requirement.',
        'This still needs interpretation: are protruding tabs included, and may the model be rotated? State those conditions rather than leaving the checker to guess. More words are useful only when they remove a real ambiguity.'],
      ['Translate a requirement into mathematics',
        'A fictional carrier with 6 kg of fixed equipment and n modules of 2 kg each has mass M = 6 + 2n. Under a 20 kg allocation, 6 + 2n ≤ 20 gives n ≤ 7. A count also requires n to be a nonnegative integer.',
        'The equation links the requirement to a calculation. It does not show that the allocation is sensible or that all equipment has been counted. Requirements and accounting boundaries need their own justification.'],
      ['Keep the reason and the check',
        'NASA’s handbook includes requirements-validation and verification guidance. At beginner scale, record why the requirement exists and how you would check it. Avoid adding arbitrary numerical limits merely to make a statement look technical.',
        'If the requirement changes, revisit the calculations that depend on it. A clean answer to an outdated limit is not a current answer. This is one reason engineers care about traceable changes and versioned information.']
    ],
    'Write one measurable requirement for a paper rover drawing. Include a unit, a boundary, and a checking method. Then identify one ambiguity that could make two people judge it differently.',
    'A requirement is useful when its meaning and its test are shared.',[R.requirements]),
  article('write-a-technical-note','engineering','Write a result someone else can use','Explain the decision and its limits in a few clear sentences.',
    'A calculation is often an intermediate product. Someone else may need to review it, compare it, or use it as an input. A short technical note should preserve enough context that the number does not become detached from the question it answers.',
    [
      ['Use a compact structure',
        'State the question, list the important inputs with units, name assumptions, show the relationship, and report the result. Then add one limitation. For a simple example, this can fit in a paragraph rather than a formal report.',
        'Example: “Estimate energy for a constant 12-watt load over 2.5 hours. Using E = Pt, delivered energy is 30 Wh. This excludes conversion losses and changing load.” The reader can reconstruct the arithmetic and see what is missing.'],
      ['Separate facts, assumptions, and choices',
        'If 12 watts came from a measured value, say so. If it was invented for practice, say that instead. If you chose a simplified constant-load model, distinguish the choice from a measured physical fact.',
        'When a result depends on an unresolved question, write the question near the result. Do not hide it in a distant note that a future reader is unlikely to see. Uncertainty about an input can be more consequential than the final arithmetic.'],
      ['Make review a conversation',
        'NASA’s teamwork and systems-engineering material emphasizes shared understanding across people and interfaces. You can practice this by giving your note to someone and asking them to identify the input, output, and limitation.',
        'If they cannot, revise the note. If they challenge an assumption, address the challenge rather than defending the prose. The aim is a result that can be questioned productively, not a presentation that discourages questions.']
    ],
    'Write a five-sentence note estimating one-way signal time over a hypothetical 90 million km at 300,000 km/s. Check: 300 seconds, or 5 minutes. Include what the simplified estimate omits.',
    'A useful result carries its context with it.',[R.teamwork,R.systems,R.delay]),
  article('leave-a-reproducible-trail','engineering','Leave enough information to repeat the work','Your future self is also a teammate.',
    'A correct answer that cannot be reconstructed is difficult to review or update. Reproducibility starts with ordinary habits: keep the inputs, the method, the units, the version, and a check. You can practice these habits in a notebook before using advanced simulation software.',
    [
      ['Keep inputs separate from the calculation',
        'For a fictional mass budget, list structure 8 kg, power 5 kg, instruments 4 kg, and communication 3 kg. Then show the total of 20 kg separately. If an instrument estimate changes to 6 kg, you can identify the changed input and the new 22 kg total.',
        'Do not silently overwrite the old value if you want to compare designs. A note such as “Revision B: instrument mass changed from 4 to 6 kg” explains why the answer changed.'],
      ['Record the method at the right detail',
        'For paper arithmetic, a readable equation may be sufficient. For a spreadsheet, keep column labels and formulas. For optional code, keep the input values and a small known-answer check. The record should let another person reproduce the result without guessing your intentions.',
        'A screenshot of the final answer alone usually loses important information. If you use a graph, preserve the expressions and axis settings as well as the picture. If you use random data in a later project, preserving the generation method will matter too.'],
      ['Control changes deliberately',
        'NASA’s configuration-management guidance concerns keeping versions and product information consistent. Your small notebook does not need the machinery of a flight project, but it can follow the same basic principle: make meaningful changes visible.',
        'When a result surprises you, compare the input and method with the previous version. This helps distinguish a real design change from an accidental edit, a unit mismatch, or a different assumption.']
    ],
    'Create two versions of a four-item mass budget, changing exactly one input. Keep both totals and a one-sentence change note. Ask someone to reproduce the difference without additional explanation.',
    'A result is easier to trust when its path can be reconstructed.',[R.management]),
  article('report-what-the-evidence-says','engineering','Report what the evidence says','Honesty includes the inconvenient uncertainty.',
    'Technical integrity begins with small choices: labeling a hypothetical value, acknowledging help, retaining an unexpected result, and stating when a check is incomplete. You do not need a high-stakes project to practice reporting the boundary between what you know and what you hope.',
    [
      ['Do not round away a decision',
        'Suppose an invented requirement is mass no greater than 25.0 kg. A stated estimate is 24.96 kg, with an assumed possible deviation of ±0.10 kg. Rounding the estimate to 25.0 does not establish that every possible value satisfies the limit.',
        'Under those illustrative bounds, mass could reach 25.06 kg. The honest conclusion is that the information given does not establish compliance across the whole assumed range. This is a reasoning exercise, not a rule for assigning uncertainty to real measurements.'],
      ['Preserve the difference between attempt and result',
        'If you solved a question with a full solution visible, record it as assisted. If a simulation has not been compared with a known case, say that verification is incomplete. If you changed an input to explore a scenario, do not present the new value as a measurement.',
        'These distinctions help you learn because they keep the next action visible. Concealing a gap may produce a cleaner-looking record while making future decisions worse. A specific limitation is often more useful than an unjustified claim of certainty.'],
      ['Treat correction as part of the work',
        'NASA identifies integrity among its core values. In a beginner project, that can mean updating a conclusion after discovering an error and explaining which result changed. It can also mean crediting a source or collaborator rather than presenting borrowed work as independent.',
        'You can be confident in your effort while cautious about a particular conclusion. The goal is neither constant self-doubt nor certainty on demand. It is a report that stays proportional to the evidence available.']
    ],
    'Write two versions of a conclusion for the 24.96 kg example: one that overclaims and one that accurately reports the assumed range. Identify the exact words that make the first misleading.',
    'Trustworthy technical work makes the limits of its evidence visible.',[R.values,R.uncertainty])
];
